      "title": "Strong approximate innerness of finite abelian diagonal actions on Cuntz-Krieger algebras",
      "description": "<p>Every finite abelian diagonal quasi-free action on a simple purely infinite Cuntz-Krieger algebra associated with a finite essential 0-1 matrix is strongly approximately inner, with implementing unitaries in the fixed algebra of the whole action. This gives an affirmative deduction for the historical Question 1 in OWR2017/42, including periodic matrices, nonouter actions and nonfaithful actions. No approximate representation law is asserted.</p><p>This note is attributed exposition and complete explicit verification of known consequences of prior theorems, not a claim of a new solution or mechanism. The main deduction uses Gabe-Szabo, arXiv:2205.04933v1 (10 May 2022), Theorem 5.8, together with the established diagonal-coboundary mechanism, an explicitly normalized vertex inner kernel, an honest cocycle, and finite-group averaging with polar correction. The separate verification material also retains the original Barlak-Szabo 2019 route with explicit reductions removing its outerness and aperiodicity restrictions. Prior credit includes Izumi for the standard finite Cuntz case (inspected 2002 preprint, Proposition 5.6(2)), Matsumoto 2000 for diagonal coboundaries and periodic-point constraints, and Crisp-Gow for the periodic return corner (inspected 2004 v1, Theorem 3.1). The source audit also credits Christensen-Neagu-Szabo, arXiv:2504.12730v1/v2, for the prior strong-approximate-innerness duality framework, without attributing the combined full-target deduction as their printed statement. The inspected preprints and the final publication metadata are distinguished. No first solution, independent discovery, new mechanism, first explicit combined theorem or current worldwide openness is claimed. Priority for an older explicit complete combined statement remains unestablished; the full Zacharias chapter and final Izumi 2004 body were unavailable in the current source audit. Bounded search failures do not certify priority.</p><p>The verification material preserves nine immutable original submitted files and a separately bound, explicitly documented current nine-file edition. Historical acceptance, source-access and aggregate-check records are not treated as current approval or reconstructed missing inputs. Written arguments cover the full fixed algebra, normalized inner kernel, honest cocycle correction, periodic return corners and nonfaithful actions. Finite graph, character, corner and cocycle programs are diagnostic consistency checks rather than proofs of operator-algebra limits; durable current programs use explicit checks in normal and both optimized Python modes. Earlier assertion-based programs remain historical evidence. Omitted older proof/program/input bytes and omitted third-party source bodies are not retroactively authenticated or replayed. AI tools were used extensively in research, mathematical reasoning, verification, literature review, internal adversarial review and writing. This work is unrefereed; no external human peer review, journal acceptance or formal proof-assistant certification is claimed.</p>",
